# Skyhook behind Caddy. Pair this with deploy/docker-compose.proxy.yml, which
# publishes the container on 127.0.0.1:4434 and speaks plain HTTP upstream.
#
# The server must be told where this listens:
#   SKYHOOK_PUBLIC_URL=https://skyhook.example.com   SKYHOOK_BEHIND_PROXY=1
#
# Caddy gets the certificate itself, upgrades WebSockets without being asked,
# and puts no idle timeout on a hijacked connection, so the whole configuration
# is one line. Two things to leave alone:
#
#   * Do not set a global read_timeout/write_timeout in the servers block. A
#     mirrored tab can sit idle for as long as its reader does, and a timeout
#     plane-side is indistinguishable from the bad link Skyhook exists to
#     survive.
#   * Do not add `encode`. Frames are already zstd-compressed; re-compressing
#     costs latency and saves nothing.
#
# Caddy serves this origin over HTTP/3 to the browser, which is a fine thing to
# have — but it is not WebTransport, and it is not the QUIC transport Skyhook
# uses when it is reached directly. The mirror still rides the WebSocket.

skyhook.example.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4434
}
